'''Lake Piríkui''' (from [[W:Old Tupi|Old Tupi]] ''Pirí Ku'i''; {{Literal translation|rustling reeds}} {{Langx|bl|Lago Piríkui}}; {{IPA|bl|la.go piˈri.kui̯|pron}}) is a [[W:Freshwater lake|freshwater lake]] nestled between [[Carpinchera]], the most populous region of [[Bayara]], and [[Camaca]]. It is the second-largest natural freshwater lake in Balisca, after [[Llanquihue]] (in Cerdanya), and the third-largest body of water overall, after the [[W:Artificial reservoir|artificial reservoir]] [[Aipi]]. 

Lake Piríkui is situated within the broad, flat [[Los Cañaverales|Bayaran coastal plain]], primarily influenced by a tropical to subtropical climate. The lake originated as an extensive [[W:Coastal lagoon|coastal lagoon]], formed when oceanic processes (waves and currents) constructed elongated sandy [[W:Barrier island|barrier islands]] or spits parallel to the main coastline. These natural barriers progressively enclosed a large, shallow body of seawater. Numerous rivers originating from the inland highlands of [[Atlántida]]'s [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] continuously flowed into the lake, and this constant influx of freshwater gradually diluted the trapped seawater, transforming the lagoon into a predominantly freshwater lake.

The inflowing rivers also carried significant quantities of sediment, which have accumulated over millennia within the lake basin. This sedimentation has contributed to its characteristically shallow depth and the formation of its many low-lying islands, marshlands, and intricate deltaic features, giving the lake its irregular shape. While largely enclosed, the lake maintains multiple, narrow and often shifting channels that connect it to the open ocean (via the city of [[Macaio]]) on its western side. 

